To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebApi, you can use the `ClaimsPrincipal` class to extract the claim containing the username. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Claims;
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;

public class MyController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly TokenHandler _tokenHandler;

    public MyController(TokenHandler tokenHandler)
    {
        _tokenHandler = tokenHandler;
    }

    [HttpGet("username")]
    public string GetUsername()
    {
        var token = Request.Headers["Authorization"].ToString().Split(' ')[1];
        var principal = _tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(token);
        var usernameClaim = principal.FindFirst(ClaimTypes.Name);

        if (usernameClaim != null)
        {
            return usernameClaim.Value;
        }
        else
        {
            return "Unknown user";
        }
    }
}
```

Make sure to inject the `TokenHandler` instance into your controller constructor. You can create an instance of `TokenHandler` like this:

```csharp
public TokenHandler(IConfiguration configuration)
{
    var key = configuration["JwtKey"];
    var algorithm = new HmacSHA256();
    algorithm.Key = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(key);
    _tokenHandler = new TokenHandler(algorithm);
}
```

Replace `"JwtKey"` with the actual key used to sign the JWT token.

