To call a PowerShell script from Terraform and allow read-host input, you can use the `local-exec` provisioner in Terraform. Here's an example:

1. Create a PowerShell script (e.g., `read_input.ps1`) with the following content:
```powershell
$input = Read-Host "Enter some input:"
Write-Host "You entered: $input"
```
1. In your Terraform configuration file (e.g., `main.tf`), add the following code to call the PowerShell script using `local-exec`:
```hcl
resource "null_resource" "read_input" {
  triggers = {
    always = "${timestamp()}"
  }

  connection {
    type        = "ssh"
    user        = "your_username"
    private_key = "${file("~/.ssh/your_private_key")}"
    host        = "your_server_ip"
    timeout     = "2m"
  }

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File read_input.ps1"
  }
}
```
Replace `your_username`, `your_private_key`, and `your_server_ip` with the appropriate values for your environment.

When you apply this Terraform configuration, it will execute the PowerShell script and prompt for input. The output of the script will be displayed in the Terraform console.

