Here's a PowerShell script that recursively checks folders for ACLs referring to SIDs in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain:

```powershell
# Define the function to check ACLs for SIDHistory references
function CheckACLs($folderPath) {
    $acl = Get-Acl -Path $folderPath
    foreach ($access in $acl.Access) {
        if ($access.IdentityReference -match '^S-1-5-21-(.*)$') {
            $sid = $matches[1]
            $sidHistory = (Get-ADUser -Filter "SamAccountName -eq '$sid'" -Properties SIDHistory).SIDHistory
            if ($sidHistory -ne $null) {
                Write-Host "Folder: $folderPath"
                Write-Host "SID: $sid"
                Write-Host "SIDHistory: $sidHistory"
            }
        }
    }
}

# Define the folder path to start with
$folderPath = "C:\Path\To\Your\Folder"

# Recursively check subfolders
function CheckSubfolders($folderPath) {
    Get-ChildItem -Path $folderPath -Directory | ForEach-Object {
        CheckACLs($_.FullName)
        CheckSubfolders($_.FullName)
    }
}

# Start the recursive check
CheckSubfolders($folderPath)
```

Replace `C:\Path\To\Your\Folder` with the root folder you want to start checking. This script will recursively check all subfolders and print out any ACLs that reference SIDs in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain.
